    useCases: ['Automated accounts payable & invoice approval', 'Cross-platform inventory & order sync', 'Employee onboarding & HR compliance workflows', 'Automated customer reporting & data feeds'],
    guideTitle: "RPA and Document Automation: What to Automate First and What It Costs",
    guide: [{"heading": "Which processes are worth automating", "content": "The best candidates are tasks that happen many times a week, follow clear rules and move data between systems: typing invoice details into accounting software, copying orders from email or marketplaces into an ERP, creating customer records from web forms, sending reminders and producing the same weekly report. We list your repetitive tasks, estimate the hours each one takes and start with the one that saves the most time with the least risk."}, {"heading": "RPA, APIs or AI document extraction?", "content": "If your systems have APIs, we connect them directly, which is the fastest and most reliable option. When an older system has no API, robotic process automation (RPA) works the screens the way a person would. For invoices, receipts, purchase orders and forms that arrive as PDFs or scans, AI document extraction reads the fields, checks them against rules and sends anything unclear to a person for review."}, {"heading": "Keeping a person in the loop", "content": "Automation should not run blind. Every workflow logs each run, retries temporary failures and sends an alert by email, Slack or WhatsApp when something needs attention. Approvals, such as paying an invoice above a set amount, stay with your team; the automation prepares the data and waits for a click."}, {"heading": "Tools we use", "content": "We build with Python, Node.js, Make, Zapier and n8n for workflows, OCR and large language models for document reading, and direct database or SFTP connections for older ERP and accounting systems. The choice depends on your volume, budget and whether you want to edit the workflows yourself later."}, {"heading": "Timeline and cost", "content": "A single workflow, such as invoice capture into your accounting software, usually takes one to three weeks. A set of connected workflows across sales, finance and operations takes four to eight weeks. You receive a fixed price after we map the process, and running costs for tools and AI usage are shown upfront."}],
